L420: ### B.2 TPR@FPR
L421: 
L422: We report TRP@FPR for detection purpose, where we introduce a threshold $\tau\in[0,1]$ on the bit accuracy. An image is classified as watermarked if ${Acc}(wm,\hat{wm})>\tau$. Different choices of $\tau$ lead to different true positive rates (TPR) and false positive rates (FPR).
L423: In diffusion watermarking, we typically cannot directly measure the FPR, because we only observe positive (watermarked) samples in practice. Following GS [cite69†50 ], for negative (non-watermarked) samples, we assume that their extracted watermark bits $\hat{wm}_{1},\hat{wm}_{2},...,\hat{wm}_{k}$ are independently and identically distributed, where each bit follows a Bernoulli distribution $Ber(0.5)$.
L424: Under this assumption, the bit accuracy of negative (non-watermarked) samples follows a binomial distribution $B(k,0.5)$. Therefore, we can use the binomial distribution to approximate the FPR as
L425:  | $\displaystyle\mathrm{FPR}(\tau)$  | $\displaystyle=\mathbb{P}(Acc(wm,\hat{wm})>\tau)$  |  | (10)
L426:  |  | $\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2^{k}}\sum_{i=\tau+1}^{k}\binom{k}{i}$  |
L427:  |  | $\displaystyle=\beta_{1/2}(\tau+1,k-\tau),$  |
L428: 
L429: where $\beta_{1/2}(\cdot,\cdot)$ is a regularized incomplete beta function.
L430: Finally, we compute TPR@FPR as follows: 1) Choose the desired FPR. 2) Solve cite256†Eq. 10 to obtain the corresponding threshold $\tau$. 3) Evaluate the proportion of watermarked images whose bit accuracies exceed the threshold $\tau$, which corresponds to the empirical TPR, defined as
L431: 
L432:  | $$\mathrm{TPR}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}^{N}\mathbf{1}(Acc(wm_{i},\hat{wm}_{i})>\tau).$$  |  | (11)
L433: In our experiments, we fix FPR=1e-6. The resulting thresholds $\tau$ for all $f_{hw}$ configurations are listed in cite257†Tab. 12 (noted that $k=\frac{c\cdot h\cdot w}{f_{hw}^{2}}$).
L434: 
